WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.420
Thomas Jane actually stabbed a guy in the chest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:08.160
and Joe Pesci gave Macauley Culkin a&nbsp;
permanent scar…with his teeth. 

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:13.080
Think&nbsp;acting is all fake? These actors have&nbsp;
the hospital bills to prove it isn't.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:17.880
It's tough to be James Bond. Sure, you&nbsp;
get the cool cars, the high-tech gadgets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:23.040
and the slick tuxedos, but you also have to&nbsp;
contend with any number of villainous henchmen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.142
assassins, and thugs prepared to&nbsp;
batter you senseless at every turn.

00:00:27.142 --> 00:00:31.200
Stunt work on Bond films has always been&nbsp;
intense, but the franchise's fight scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.760
have grown particularly brutal in their&nbsp;
execution during Daniel Craig's tenure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.820
and he's got the wounds to&nbsp;
prove it. During the filming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:42.540
of "Spectre," Craig went toe-to-toe with&nbsp;
professional wrestler-turned-actor Dave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:46.555
Bautista during a fight scene that was&nbsp;
supposed to take place on a moving train.

00:00:46.555 --> 00:00:51.420
At one point, Bautista threw Craig across the&nbsp;
train car, and while the move was planned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:56.640
the way Craig landed was not. Craig tore&nbsp;
a meniscus in his knee and had to take two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.860
weeks off from filming for arthroscopic surgery.&nbsp;
He doesn't blame Bautista, though, and in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:06.725
he accidentally got his revenge by breaking&nbsp;
Bautista's nose when they later reshot the scene.

00:01:06.725 --> 00:01:09.420
"I threw this punch. I hit him on&nbsp;
the nose and I heard this crack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.318
and I went 'Oh God no' and ran away."

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.800
Professional wrestling legend Jerry "The King"&nbsp;
Lawler once famously feuded with the iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.960
and infamously confrontational comedian&nbsp;
Andy Kaufman. Sixteen years after that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:27.720
Lawler was called upon to recreate the feud&nbsp;
by playing himself in the Kaufman biopic "Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:31.860
on the Moon." This time, Lawler would be&nbsp;
acting opposite star Jim Carrey, who was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:37.260
only supposed to be pretending to be Kaufman.&nbsp;
Carrey took his role very seriously, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:43.260
and things got real for Lawler once again — even&nbsp;
more real than they had ever been with Kaufman.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:48.600
As covered in the documentary "Jim &amp; Andy: The&nbsp;
Great Beyond," Carrey was relentlessly devoted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.980
playing Kaufman, remaining in character during&nbsp;
the entire production of "Man on the Moon" to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:58.320
the point that he puzzled and often frustrated&nbsp;
his collaborators. Things sometimes got heated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.040
and that included scenes with Lawler.&nbsp;
At one point during the production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:07.740
Carrey — in character as Kaufman — spit at the&nbsp;
wrestler, prompting Lawler to get physical. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:12.725
exact nature of the injury isn't clear, but it&nbsp;
was enough to briefly land Carrey in a hospital.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.725 --> 00:02:18.131
Even years later, Lawler was still expressing&nbsp;
his unease at how far Carrey took everything.

00:02:18.131 --> 00:02:20.231
"Well, Jim Carrey's an idiot."

00:02:20.231 --> 00:02:24.420
Though the "Rocky" films are often very&nbsp;
melodramatic and bombastic in their portrayal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:29.400
of the title character's story, creator Sylvester&nbsp;
Stallone still always wanted to aim for some level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:34.740
of realism during the making of the series. In&nbsp;
"Rocky IV," in which his character faces Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:40.440
boxer Ivan Drago on a quest to avenge the death of&nbsp;
his friend Apollo Creed, Stallone drew inspiration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:45.485
from a real-life boxing match featuring two&nbsp;
fighters who hated each other outside of the ring.

00:02:45.485 --> 00:02:50.640
Reasoning that Rocky and Drago would also hate&nbsp;
each other, Stallone instructed actor Dolph&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:54.300
Lundgren to actually hit him during&nbsp;
the opening moments of their fight.

00:02:54.462 --> 00:02:56.302
"I must break you."

00:02:57.993 --> 00:03:03.540
As requested, Lundgren came out swinging…and&nbsp;
he hit Stallone so hard in the chest that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:08.160
had to cut the scene short. Stallone knew the&nbsp;
punches were serious, but he didn't know how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:12.600
serious until later that night when his blood&nbsp;
pressure shot up and he had to be flown from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:18.000
Canada back to the United States for emergency&nbsp;
medical treatment. Stallone later explained:

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.320
"He hit my heart so hard that it banged&nbsp;
against my ribs and started to swell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:25.162
and that usually happens in car accidents."

00:03:25.162 --> 00:03:29.940
Actors understand that injuries can happen&nbsp;
during intense sequences, particularly when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:35.160
the make-believe of a scene involves a physical&nbsp;
struggle. Sometimes, though, the real drama of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:40.620
a moment lives on long after it's committed to&nbsp;
film. This was the case with "Gothika," the 2003&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:45.425
thriller that earned a lot of coverage in the&nbsp;
entertainment press thanks to an on-set injury.

00:03:45.425 --> 00:03:49.020
During a scene in which her character,&nbsp;
Dr. Miranda Grey, is being interrogated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:53.100
star Halle Berry was supposed to struggle&nbsp;
against co-star Robert Downey Jr.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:58.200
who was supposed to attempt to restrain her.&nbsp;
Berry approached the scene with such intensity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:02.820
that Downey accidentally broke a bone in her&nbsp;
forearm while trying to hold her back. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:07.320
injury made headlines, and Downey was frequently&nbsp;
asked — to the point of frustration — to explain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:12.453
what happened while he tried to promote the&nbsp;
film. In one of those interviews, he told IGN:

00:04:12.453 --> 00:04:17.040
"I would say to her, 'Listen. You're badass,&nbsp;
but you weigh 102 pounds and your bones are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.940
as thin as, like, Crayolas. [...] Then&nbsp;
I turn out to be the a—hole who's like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:24.600
trying to put her arm down with my&nbsp;
hand open and I feel her ulna snap."

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:29.460
Channing Tatum received some of the best reviews&nbsp;
of his career for director Bennett Miller's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:35.280
2014 drama "Foxcatcher," which was based on true&nbsp;
events that happened at John du Pont's self-funded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:40.680
wrestling training facility in the 1990s. As&nbsp;
Olympic wrestler Mark Schultz, Tatum worked hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:46.560
to reach a certain level of realism and intensity&nbsp;
in each scene, and it sometimes cost him. In one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.960
memorable moment, Tatum slammed his head so hard&nbsp;
through a prop mirror that he hit the wall behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:57.600
it, leaving him with a cut. In another, Tatum's&nbsp;
demand for intensity led to yet another injury —&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:03.158
this time at the hands of co-star Mark Ruffalo,&nbsp;
who played Mark's brother and coach Dave Schultz.

00:05:03.158 --> 00:05:08.040
For a scene in which Dave slaps Mark upside&nbsp;
the head, Tatum asked Ruffalo to hit him for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:13.347
real. Ruffalo did, but Tatum immediately&nbsp;
regretted his request. Tatum told Variety:

00:05:13.347 --> 00:05:18.971
"He pops my eardrum. All of a sudden it's just&nbsp;
making a screeching noise. I can't hear anything."

00:05:19.107 --> 00:05:22.560
Tatum's eardrum ultimately healed,&nbsp;
but the look of pain on his face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.967
in the finished film after Ruffalo&nbsp;
slapped him was definitely not acting.

00:05:26.967 --> 00:05:30.540
AMC's massive zombie hit "The&nbsp;
Walking Dead" is an intense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:34.380
show full of shocking death scenes&nbsp;
and undead battles. And, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:38.940
brawls between its often at-odds human&nbsp;
characters. As the show's leading man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:43.860
Andrew Lincoln was often at the forefront of these&nbsp;
confrontations, and he took his job seriously.

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:48.120
According to co-star Norman Reedus, in an&nbsp;
attempt to keep his energy high, Lincoln&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:53.700
would often get very caffeinated before the show's&nbsp;
most intense scenes. This, plus Lincoln's tendency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:58.320
toward clumsiness, would sometimes lead to his&nbsp;
co-stars unceremoniously getting punched in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:02.580
the face. Reedus described one such encounter&nbsp;
on "The Tonight Show Starring Jimmy Fallon."

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:06.360
"He's supposed to run and tackle me&nbsp;
off-camera, right? Just jump into an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:10.805
airbag. But he jumps and he punches&nbsp;
me in the face with double fists."

00:06:10.805 --> 00:06:15.060
And that was apparently far from the only&nbsp;
accidental injury Lincoln caused. Reedus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:19.500
noted that Lincoln had developed quite the&nbsp;
reputation for his accidental punches. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:24.060
was such an issue that Jeffrey Dean Morgan,&nbsp;
who plays Negan on the show, called Reedus up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:28.500
one night to express his dread over shooting a&nbsp;
fight scene with Lincoln the following day. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:33.720
result? About what you'd expect, according to a&nbsp;
photo Morgan texted Reedus following the scene.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:37.129
"It's Jeff with a bandaid on his&nbsp;
nose, with an ice pack, right?"

00:06:37.129 --> 00:06:37.669
"No."

00:06:37.669 --> 00:06:39.763
"Yeah he punched him in the&nbsp;
nose, like fractured his nose."

00:06:39.763 --> 00:06:45.060
Anyone who's ever seen "Goodfellas" knows that&nbsp;
Oscar-winner Joe Pesci is an actor with a talent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:49.380
for playing very scary characters. And it's&nbsp;
a skill that Pesci himself apparently takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:54.600
quite seriously, even when he's using it for a&nbsp;
light family comedy. In the holiday classic "Home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:59.520
Alone," Pesci plays Harry, the smarter and more&nbsp;
savage of the two bandits who attempt to terrorize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:04.620
and rob Macaulay Culkin's Kevin McCallister, only&nbsp;
to find that he's more than they bargained for.

00:07:08.155 --> 00:07:10.915
"You guys give up, or you thirsty for more?"

00:07:10.915 --> 00:07:14.820
Though he's ultimately playing an&nbsp;
overconfident buffoon, Pesci still felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:18.300
he needed to make Culkin uncomfortable&nbsp;
during their scenes together. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:22.860
he deliberately kept his distance from the young&nbsp;
star on set until it was time to actually play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:27.600
the scary bad guy. But even Pesci didn't mean&nbsp;
to go quite as far as he did in one scene.

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:32.460
During their final confrontation after the bandits&nbsp;
catch Kevin and prepare to pay him back for all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:37.080
the pain he's caused them, Harry threatens to&nbsp;
bite off all of the boy's fingers. In the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:41.220
he never actually gets to, as Kevin is&nbsp;
saved at the last moment. In real life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:45.889
Pesci accidentally bit Culkin during&nbsp;
one take and left a permanent scar.

00:07:45.889 --> 00:07:48.540
"It broke the skin and everything. I'm&nbsp;
a little nine-year-old boy and he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:51.491
going around biting them. I still have the scar."

00:07:51.491 --> 00:07:56.460
Most of the time when an actor injures one of&nbsp;
their co-stars, it's an accident. Other times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:00.960
it's the product of said co-star demanding&nbsp;
to be struck for the sake of realism. Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:04.080
there are those rare moments, like one&nbsp;
that happened on the set of "The Texas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:07.980
Chain Saw Massacre," when someone just&nbsp;
really wants to get the scene over with.

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:12.240
The famous "dinner" scene in Leatherface's&nbsp;
house in "The Texas Chain Saw Massacre" was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:16.680
filmed in one marathon shooting day in an&nbsp;
un-air-conditioned house in the relentless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:21.480
summer heat. The actors were covered in sweat,&nbsp;
the stench in the room was overpowering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:26.400
and everyone involved was beginning to go a little&nbsp;
crazy as they were pushed to their working limits.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:31.680
This craziness ultimately manifested itself as&nbsp;
a real injury during the moment in which Gunnar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:36.240
Hansen's Leatherface cuts Sally's finger&nbsp;
to feed her blood to Grandpa. The blade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:40.020
of the knife was covered with tape to make&nbsp;
it dull, and Hansen was supposed to pretend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:44.940
to cut actress Marilyn Burns, then squeeze a&nbsp;
bulb full of fake blood concealed in his hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:49.620
to make it look like Burns was bleeding.&nbsp;
According to a 2003 DVD commentary track,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:54.120
when the tube that was supposed to squirt&nbsp;
out the blood kept clogging, Hansen got so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:59.536
frustrated that he pulled the tape off and cut&nbsp;
Burns for real, just to get the scene over with.

00:08:59.536 --> 00:09:04.800
In 1995, the "Mortal Kombat" film roundhouse&nbsp;
kicked its way into theaters and into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:09.900
hearts of moviegoers around the globe. While&nbsp;
expectations were low — especially since the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:15.360
video game movie curse was still in full effect —&nbsp;
fans were blown away by the fierce and thrilling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:19.800
action on display. Part of the reason the&nbsp;
film worked is because director Paul W. S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:24.720
Anderson employed actual fighting experts in&nbsp;
pivotal parts. For the role of the spectral,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:30.240
fire-breathing ninja Scorpion, Anderson hired&nbsp;
martial artist and stuntman Chris Casamassa.

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:33.060
One of the best fight scenes&nbsp;
of the film takes place between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:36.300
Linden Ashby's Johnny Cage&nbsp;
and Casamassa's Scorpion.

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:37.620
"Get over here."

00:09:39.796 --> 00:09:44.520
The two begin their battle in a forest before&nbsp;
being transported to another eerie dimension,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:49.740
concluding the bout in fiery fashion.&nbsp;
It's a brutal exchange of fists and kicks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:54.322
and one of Casamassa's attacks actually&nbsp;
left Ashby in considerable pain.

00:09:54.322 --> 00:09:57.840
Speaking to The Hollywood Reporter,&nbsp;
Ashby explained how Casamassa helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:01.936
him prepare for the fight scenes, but he&nbsp;
said there was an unintentional accident.

00:10:01.936 --> 00:10:06.540
"Chris did an ax kick to my kidneys in&nbsp;
that fight. I had a pad on but his heel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:12.535
just came right between the pads and got me in the&nbsp;
kidney, hard. I was peeing blood. It hurt a lot."

00:10:12.535 --> 00:10:15.867
Not quite a fatality, but definitely painful.

00:10:16.031 --> 00:10:20.460
As the star of 2010's "The Expendables,"&nbsp;
Sylvester Stallone got to test his mettle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:25.500
alongside other action heroes like Dolph&nbsp;
Lundgren, Jet Li, and Jason Statham. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:29.455
it wasn't any of them who caused&nbsp;
damage to Sly this time around.

00:10:29.455 --> 00:10:31.440
"Only an idiot would do this job."

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:32.160
"How much?"

00:10:32.296 --> 00:10:33.360
"Like I said."

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:40.920
During the movie, Stallone's Barney Ross squares&nbsp;
off against "Stone Cold" Steve Austin's aptly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:45.600
named character, Paine. Austin, of course,&nbsp;
is well known for his mudhole-stomping and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:50.880
Stone Cold Stunners in the WWE. It appears&nbsp;
as if the Texas Rattlesnake brought the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:55.440
energy to his performance here, but it left&nbsp;
Stallone a little worse for wear in the end.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:00.180
Speaking to FHM Magazine, Stallone revealed&nbsp;
the intensity of the fight scenes in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:03.180
including one that left him&nbsp;
needing surgery afterward.

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:07.440
"Actually, my fight with Stone Cold&nbsp;
Steve Austin was so vicious that I ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:11.940
up getting a hairline fracture in my neck.&nbsp;
I'm not joking. I haven't told anyone this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:17.520
but I had to have a very serious operation&nbsp;
afterwards. I now have a metal plate in my neck."

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:22.664
In 2020, Halle Berry marked her directorial&nbsp;
debut with the Netflix sports drama "Bruised."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.664 --> 00:11:27.780
Berry also stars in the film as MMA fighter&nbsp;
Jackie "Pretty Bull" Justice. Expectedly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:31.260
she gets the opportunity to show&nbsp;
what she is made of in a few scenes.

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:37.200
In "Bruised," Justice's main opponent is Lucia&nbsp;
"Lady Killer" Chavez — portrayed by real-life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:42.720
UFC fighter Valentina Shevchenko. Speaking&nbsp;
to Sports Illustrated, Berry revealed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:46.800
Shevchenko played a major part in ensuring&nbsp;
she was prepared for the film by training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:51.120
her. The actor also said that they decided to&nbsp;
film their big fight early in the production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:56.471
which was when Berry quickly found out just&nbsp;
how painful MMA can really be. Berry said:

00:11:56.471 --> 00:12:01.380
"On Day 2, I got kicked and broke two ribs. And,&nbsp;
you know, there was a moment when I thought:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:06.180
'We can shut this down and I can go heal, or I&nbsp;
can keep going and just fight my way through it.'"

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:09.660
However, the actor decided against&nbsp;
it, considering all the time that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:13.860
both she and Shevchenko had invested in the&nbsp;
training, as well as how a delay could result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:18.300
in the entire film being canceled. So she&nbsp;
pushed through the pain to film the fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:23.007
saying she waited until they had the scene&nbsp;
before telling anyone about her injury.

00:12:23.007 --> 00:12:27.300
For Ryan Coogler's "Creed," a decision was&nbsp;
made to take the realism to the next level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:32.340
by casting real boxers in the film. Michael&nbsp;
B. Jordan's Adonis Creed steps into the ring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:36.420
against "Pretty" Ricky Conlan, who is&nbsp;
portrayed by real-life champion boxer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.420 --> 00:12:40.980
Tony Bellew. Much like Sylvester Stallone&nbsp;
took a proper hook from Dolph Lundgren&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:45.715
in "Rocky IV," Jordan tasted a knuckle&nbsp;
sandwich in the movie, courtesy of Bellew.

00:12:45.715 --> 00:12:48.960
Appearing on "The Graham Norton Show,"&nbsp;
Jordan revealed that there was a need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:52.920
get footage of him taking a punch for&nbsp;
a slow-motion sequence. There is no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:56.770
way to fake that type of scene so he had to&nbsp;
take a real punch from his co-star — twice.

00:12:57.752 --> 00:12:58.252
"And, action."

00:12:59.274 --> 00:13:09.660
While Jordan said he didn't quite get&nbsp;
knocked out, he did feel the impact, adding:

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:13.860
"It felt like I was in a car accident&nbsp;
for like four days afterward. Tony said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:17.940
he only went 40 percent; it&nbsp;
felt like 85. It was tough."

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:22.080
Compared to other films mentioned here,&nbsp;
Francis Ford Coppola's "The Godfather"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:25.620
isn't exactly bursting with intense&nbsp;
action that should result in broken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:29.820
bones and busted lips. Sure, there's&nbsp;
violence on display, but there should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:34.282
be no reason for the actors to suffer any&nbsp;
on-set injuries apart from a freak accident.

00:13:34.282 --> 00:13:38.820
However, the memorable scene where Sonny&nbsp;
Corleone lays into his brother-in-law,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:43.538
Carlo, carries a bit more bite because of&nbsp;
the real-life bad blood between the actors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.538 --> 00:13:48.660
Apparently James Caan and Gianni Russo&nbsp;
had developed some hostility toward each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:52.980
other throughout the production of the 1972&nbsp;
film. They still rehearsed their big scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:56.160
but when it came down to the day&nbsp;
of the shoot, Caan started to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.160 --> 00:14:00.540
things differently and more viciously.&nbsp;
Russo explained to Entertainment Weekly:

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:04.440
"He improvised a few things like that&nbsp;
little billy club he threw at me when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:08.220
I came off the stoop. He hit me right in&nbsp;
the head with that, and then he throws me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:12.000
over the railing and he's biting my hands.&nbsp;
[...] None of that was supposed to happen."

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:17.105
"And when I crawl out, he literally&nbsp;
lifted me up with his kick."

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:20.700
Russo added that the beating left him with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:24.125
injuries including broken&nbsp;
ribs and a chipped elbow.

00:14:24.125 --> 00:14:29.940
The story of 1989's "Cyborg" merits a book.&nbsp;
While it might appear like one of The Cannon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:34.680
Group's trademark low-budget action films, there's&nbsp;
a lot of history behind how this production came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:39.480
to be. The short of it is that Cannon planned to&nbsp;
shoot "Spider-Man" and "Masters of the Universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:45.360
2" at the same time to cut costs, but plans fell&nbsp;
apart. Since Cannon had already spent the money on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:51.000
costumes and props, filmmaker Albert Pyun wrote&nbsp;
a script over a weekend that became "Cyborg."

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:55.800
Jean-Claude Van Damme signed up to play the&nbsp;
lead role of Gibson Rickenbacker, and it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.800 --> 00:15:00.660
off to the races. As with any film featuring&nbsp;
Van Damme, "Cyborg" is all about the action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:05.460
and there are countless fight scenes in the movie.&nbsp;
In one of the sequences, Van Damme's character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:11.133
battles a gang of pirates, one of whom is played&nbsp;
by former bodybuilder Jackson "Rock" Pinckney.

00:15:11.133 --> 00:15:15.633
Unfortunately, an accident occurred when&nbsp;
a prop knife went into Pickney's eye.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.633 --> 00:15:22.800
According to the Orlando Sentinel, Pickney&nbsp;
sued and was awarded $487,500 in damages.

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:29.155
Rob Reiner's "The Princess Bride" is a beloved&nbsp;
1987 fantasy film that has stood the test of time.

00:15:29.155 --> 00:15:30.535
"What can I do for you?"

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:33.420
"You can die slowly, cut into a thousand pieces."

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:39.660
It's a riveting action-adventure story that&nbsp;
follows Westley as he tries to rescue the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.660 --> 00:15:43.920
love of his life, Princess Buttercup. It&nbsp;
boasts plenty of exciting action sequences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:48.660
but the scene that knocked actor Cary&nbsp;
Elwes out cold came during a relatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:52.140
tame moment. It happened during&nbsp;
a scene in which Count Rugen puts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:56.051
Westley down for the count by bonking&nbsp;
his head with the handle of his sword.

00:15:56.051 --> 00:16:00.060
According to Elwes' book "As You Wish:&nbsp;
Inconceivable Tales from the Making of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:04.380
The Princess Bride," Christopher Guest, who&nbsp;
played Rugen, had no intention of harming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:08.940
him with his metal sword in the scene. The&nbsp;
problem was it didn't look great on camera. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:14.400
Elwes said, he encouraged Guest to "slightly&nbsp;
tap" his head and he would move. Elwes wrote:

00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:18.600
"Chris swung the heavy sword down toward&nbsp;
my head. However, as fate would have it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:22.680
it landed just a touch harder than&nbsp;
either of us anticipated. And that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.680 --> 00:16:25.958
folks, was the last thing I&nbsp;
remember from that day's shoot."

00:16:25.958 --> 00:16:31.598
"I woke up in the hospital with stitches&nbsp;
being sewn into my forehead, and the scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.598 --> 00:16:34.980
that you see in the movie is in&nbsp;
fact me being knocked out for real."

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:39.780
On a more lighthearted note, the most famous&nbsp;
injury in The Princess Bride didn't come from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:44.520
any physical altercation whatsoever. It&nbsp;
happened during the scene in which Inigo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:49.369
and Fezzik take Westley to be healed by&nbsp;
Miracle Max, played by Billy Crystal.

00:16:49.369 --> 00:16:53.580
Crystal improvised much of his material&nbsp;
as Max, churning out jokes on the set for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:58.320
three days while his co-stars fought to hold in&nbsp;
their laughter during filming. In many cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:02.640
they were unsuccessful, breaking character&nbsp;
during takes because Crystal was just too funny.

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:06.300
Mandy Patinkin in particular, though,&nbsp;
really wanted to make it through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.300 --> 00:17:09.960
scenes without laughing, and it cost&nbsp;
him. By the time filming was done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.960 --> 00:17:12.982
the effort actually gave him a physical injury.

00:17:12.982 --> 00:17:16.500
"The only injury I sustained on&nbsp;
that movie was I bruised a rib&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.500 --> 00:17:21.240
from holding in my laughter with Billy&nbsp;
Crystal, and that's God's honest truth."

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:26.100
If Sean Bean is in a movie, chances are his&nbsp;
character won't make it to the final credits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.100 --> 00:17:31.440
No matter if he's a good or bad guy, Bean's&nbsp;
on-screen personas seem to be fated to dance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:36.540
with the grim reaper under the pale moonlight.&nbsp;
In 1992's "Patriot Games," Bean's character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:41.580
Sean Miller doesn't make it out alive either —&nbsp;
unsurprisingly. Though, in this case, he is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.580 --> 00:17:46.560
villain, so no one can be too upset about Harrison&nbsp;
Ford's Jack Ryan putting him in a watery grave.

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:51.720
Miller and Ryan have an exhilarating final&nbsp;
encounter on a moving speedboat. Throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.720 --> 00:17:55.860
the clash, Ryan looks to be on the back&nbsp;
foot and overpowered by Miller; however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.860 --> 00:18:00.660
the CIA's favorite child perseveres and&nbsp;
puts his opponent down once and for all.

00:18:00.660 --> 00:18:05.340
As Bean revealed on The Compleat Sean Bean,&nbsp;
the scuffle between him and Ford resulted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:08.820
in him receiving the infamous scar on&nbsp;
his face. While shooting the scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:13.936
Ford accidentally hit him with a boat hook,&nbsp;
which cut just under Bean's left eyebrow.

00:18:13.936 --> 00:18:18.300
"The Punisher" is finally getting its&nbsp;
flowers years after its 2004 release.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:23.880
Starring Thomas Jane as Frank Castle, it is a&nbsp;
bloody good time that doesn't shirk away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:28.140
violent encounters and manages to capture&nbsp;
the tone of the comic book character. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:31.800
might not have the body count of Marvel's&nbsp;
next big-screen stab at the character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.800 --> 00:18:37.260
2008's "Punisher: War Zone," but it did&nbsp;
feature its own literal on-set stabbing.

00:18:37.260 --> 00:18:41.640
Appearing on "The Rich Eisen Show," Jane revealed&nbsp;
that someone had forgotten to replace the real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:46.260
knife with the prop. So during the scene where&nbsp;
Castle fights the hulking Russian — played by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.260 --> 00:18:51.344
ex-pro wrestler Kevin Nash — Jane stabs&nbsp;
Nash in the chest with an actual knife.

00:18:51.344 --> 00:18:55.364
"It stabs him, and it goes in, and I'm&nbsp;
looking at the knife, I'm looking at him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.364 --> 00:18:59.460
Kevin, and he's just looking down at me, and&nbsp;
there's a knife sticking out of his chest."

00:18:59.460 --> 00:19:03.180
The actor said he expected Nash&nbsp;
to lash out and react in anger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.180 --> 00:19:07.140
but Nash didn't. Nonetheless, Jane&nbsp;
said he still felt terrible about it.

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:10.680
For his part, Nash has said that he doesn't&nbsp;
hold any grudges about what happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.680 --> 00:19:13.680
but he says that the people&nbsp;
in charge of props should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:17.400
doing their jobs more diligently to avoid&nbsp;
these types of incidents from occurring.

00:19:17.400 --> 00:19:19.680
"He just sat there and looked at the knife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:25.260
looked at me. Shook his head. I&nbsp;
sent him a couple cases of beer."

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:30.360
"Bloodsport" is widely seen as the film&nbsp;
that catapulted Jean-Claude Van Damme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:34.620
into superstardom, and it's also recognized&nbsp;
as one of the greatest martial arts movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.620 --> 00:19:38.820
of all time. While most fans remember&nbsp;
"Bloodsport" for the vicious final fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.820 --> 00:19:43.200
there is another swift battle that stands&nbsp;
out — albeit for a different reason.

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:47.640
An earlier fight scene with actor Bernard&nbsp;
Mariano ends with him making one last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.640 --> 00:19:51.960
attempt to get Van Damme, who puts him&nbsp;
back down with a chop to the face. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.960 --> 00:19:56.133
scene looks great on film; however,&nbsp;
it wasn't the best time for Mariano.

00:19:56.133 --> 00:19:59.476
Speaking to South China Morning&nbsp;
Post, Mariano explained:

00:19:59.476 --> 00:20:03.780
"When we rolled the first take, he moved too&nbsp;
fast. [...] He was already moving back and caught&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.780 --> 00:20:09.703
me full on the jaw and split my lip. I passed&nbsp;
out and was taken to hospital and stitched up."

00:20:09.703 --> 00:20:15.273
Mariano insisted that the blood on the mat, which&nbsp;
appears in the final cut, was actually his own.

